        "rapidmlx/qwen3.6:35b-a3b",
        "--title",
        "comparison launch",
        "p-limit's limitFunction(function_, options) in index.js returns a function that runs function_ with limited concurrency, and its options accept rejectOnClear just as pLimit's do, but the returned function offers no way to discard the calls still waiting to run, so that option has no effect there. Today limitFunction(async () => 'done', {concurrency: 1}).clearQueue is undefined, and calling it throws \"TypeError: limited.clearQueue is not a function\". Give the returned function a clearQueue() method that discards the calls that have not started yet, exactly as clearQueue() does for a pLimit limiter: it returns undefined; calls already running are not cancelled and settle as usual; the function of a discarded call is never invoked; with rejectOnClear: true each discarded call's promise rejects with an error whose name is \"AbortError\", and without it (the default) the discarded promises stay unsettled; calls made after clearing run normally, and calls that are still running keep counting against the concurrency limit. For example, with const limited = limitFunction(async x => { await delay(40); return x * 2; }, {concurrency: 1, rejectOnClear: true}), calling limited(1), limited(2) and limited(3) and then limited.clearQueue() gives 2 for the first call and AbortError rejections for the other two, whose function never runs. Clearing one limited function does not affect another one, even when both were made with the same options object, and clearQueue() on a limited function with nothing waiting is harmless. The TypeScript declarations in index.d.ts must describe the method: for const lf = limitFunction(async (_a: string) => 'ok', {concurrency: 1}), lf.clearQueue() must type-check with type void, and lf('input') must stay Promise<string>. What must not change: the limited function forwards all of its arguments and resolves with the function's result (or rejects with its error, a synchronous throw included); it never runs more than options.concurrency calls at once; invalid options still throw a TypeError (a concurrency of 0, a rejectOnClear that is not a boolean); and the default export pLimit, with its activeCount, pendingCount, clearQueue, concurrency and map, behaves as it does today. The source is index.js, with its type declarations in index.d.ts; the tests are test.js (ava) and the type tests index.test-d.ts (tsd). Add regression tests for this behaviour to the project's existing test suite. The project's tests run with npm test (xo, then ava, then tsd).\n\nVisible acceptance check (it must pass; it is not the only criterion): node acceptance/visible/check.mjs\nThe project's own tests run with: npm test\nDeliverables, all required; the task is not done until each is in the tree:\n1. The implementation of the behaviour described above, in the project's own source.\n2. Regression tests: at least one new test case, in the project's own test directory and style, that fails on the current code and passes with your change, adding at least one new assertion. A change without a new test is incomplete.\n3. The visible acceptance check (node acceptance/visible/check.mjs) and the project's own test command (npm test) both passing on the final tree.\nAllowed changes: implementation code, and regression tests for the change (new test files, or new cases added to existing test files, in the project's own test directory and style).\nDo not modify anything under acceptance/visible/.\nDo not change how the project's checks run: leave the test and coverage configuration files (.taprc*, .nycrc*, .c8rc*, .mocharc*, jest.config.*, vitest.config.*, ava.config.*, karma.conf.*) and the test, pretest and posttest scripts and runner sections of package.json as they are.\nDo not delete or weaken existing tests: keep every existing assertion (it may move to another test file), add no skip, only or todo markers, and add no coverage-ignore pragmas. A change that does any of these is rejected at evaluation whatever else passes."
      ],
